H_{ij}$, which sums the entries of the various conjugates of $H$, obtained by switching signs on rows and columns.\n# arXiv++ Combinatorics\n\nBrowse math.CO papers from arXiv\n\n[Input: Search by title, author, BibTeX key, or arXiv URL...] [Button: Search]\n\n## Papers by Teodor Banica\n\n19 paper(s) by this author · All BibTeX\n\n2017-06-03 v2\n\nTeodor Banica, Duygu Ozteke, Lorenzo Pittau\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nIsolated partial Hadamard matrices, and related topics\n\nPublished in Open Syst. Inf. Dyn. 25 (2018), 1-27 • View Publication • BIB\n\nWe study the isolated partial Hadamard matrices, under the assumption that the entries are roots of unity, or more generally, under the assumption that the combinatorics comes from vanishing sums of roots of unity. We first review the various conjectures on the subject, and then we present several new results, regarding notably the master Hadamard matrices, and the McNulty-Weigert construction. We discuss then the notion of isolation in some related contexts, of the magic unitary matrices, and of the quantum permutation groups, with a number of conjectures on the subject.\n\n2017-05-29 v2\n\nTeodor Banica\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nComplex Hadamard matrices with noncommutative entries\n\nPublished in Ann. Funct. Anal. 9, no. 3 (2018), 354-368 • View Publication • BIB\n\nWe axiomatize and study the matrices of type $H\\in M_N(A)$, having unitary entries, $H_{ij}\\in U(A)$, and whose rows and columns are subject to orthogonality type conditions. Here $A$ can be any $C^*$-algebra, for instance $A=\\mathbb C$, where we obtain the usual complex Hadamard matrices, or $A=C(X)$, where we obtain the continuous families of complex Hadamard matrices. Our formalism allows the construction of a quantum permutation group $G\\subset S_N^+$, whose structure and computation is discussed here.\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nCombinatorial aspects of orthogonal group integrals\n\nPublished in Internat. J. Math. 22 (2011), 1611-1646 • View Publication • BIB\n\nWe study the integrals of type $I(a)=\\int_{O_n}\\prod u_{ij}^{a_{ij}}\\,du$, depending on a matrix $a\\in M_{p\\times q}(\\mathbb N)$, whose exact computation is an open problem. Our results are as follows: (1) an extension of the \"elementary expansion\" formula from the case $a\\in M_{2\\times q}(2\\mathbb N)$ to the general case $a\\in M_{p\\times q}(\\mathbb N)$, (2) the construction of the \"best algebraic normalization\" of $I(a)$, in the case $a\\in M_{2\\times q}(\\mathbb N)$, (3) an explicit formula for $I(a)$, for diagonal matrices $a\\in M_{3\\times 3}(\\mathbb N)$, (4) a modelling result in the case $a\\in M_{1\\times 2}(\\mathbb N)$, in relation with the Euler-Rodrigues formula. Most proofs use various combinatorial techniques.\n\n2009-01-19\n\nTeodor Banica, Benoît Collins, Jean-Marc Schlenker\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nOn orthogonal matrices maximizing the 1-norm\n\nPublished in Indiana Univ. Math. J. 59 (2010), no. 3, 839-856 • View Publication • BIB\n\nFor $U\\in O(N)$ we have $||U||_1\\leq N\\sqrt{N}$, with equality if and only if $U=H/\\sqrt{N}$, with $H$ Hadamard matrix. Motivated by this remark, we discuss in this paper the algebraic and analytic aspects of the computation of the maximum of the 1-norm on O(N). The main problem is to compute the $k$-th moment of the 1-norm, with $k\\to\\infty$, and we present a number of general comments in this direction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMATRIX MODELS FOR NONCOMMUTATIVE ALGEBRAIC (https://lmbp.uca.fr/~bichon/modnc-revised.pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; TEODOR BANICA AND JULIEN BICHON ... We discuss the notion of matrix model, π : C(X) →MK(C(T)), for alge-\nMATRIX MODELS FOR NONCOMMUTATIVE ALGEBRAIC\nMANIFOLDS\nTEODOR BANICA AND JULIEN BICHON\nAbstract. We discuss the notion of matrix model, π : C(X) →MK(C(T)), for alge-\nbraic submanifolds of the free complex sphere, X ⊂SN−1\nC,+ . When K ∈N is ﬁxed there is\na universal such model, which factorizes as π : C(X) →C(X(K)) ⊂MK(C(T)). We have\nX(1) = Xclass and, under a mild assumption, inclusions X(1) ⊂X(2) ⊂X(3) ⊂. . . ⊂X.\nOur main results concern X(2), X(3), X(4), . . ., their relation with various half-classical\nversions of X, and lead to the construction of families of higher half-liberations of the\ncomplex spheres and of the unitary groups, all having faithful matrix models.\nIntroduction\nThere are several possible deﬁnitions for the noncommutative algebraic manifolds. Ac-\ncording to a well-known theorem of Gelfand, one reasonable point of view is that the\nnoncommutative analogues of the compact real algebraic manifolds Xclass ⊂CN should\nbe the abstract spectra of the universal C∗-algebras of the following type:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nsanmarlat.dvi (https://emis.de/ft/48419)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; In fact, let \\(g_n \\in L\\) be a contractive sequence and write \\(g_n = u_n a_n v_n\\) for its polar decomposition.\n\n**Lemma 3.3.** *Let \\(G\\) be a topological group, \\(L \\subset G\\) a closed subgroup and \\(S \\subset G\\) a semigroup with \\(\\operatorname{int} S \\ne \\varnothing\\). We have,*\n\n1. *If \\(S\\) is transitive on \\(G/L\\) then \\(\\operatorname{int} gSg^{-1} \\cap L \\ne \\varnothing\\) for all \\(g \\in G\\).*\n\n2. *Reciprocally, suppose that \\(G/L\\) is connected. Then \\(S\\) is transitive in \\(G/L\\) if \\(\\operatorname{int} gSg^{-1} \\cap L\\) is not empty for all \\(g \\in G\\).*\n\n**Proof.** See Corollary 2.2 in [10]. ■\n\nAs mentioned above we shall prove the converse of Theorem 3.1 under the additional assumption that the action of \\(\\theta(L)\\) is contractive and minimal. At this regard we note that contractivity of the \\(L\\)-action ensures trivially that the action of \\(\\theta(L)\\) is contractive. In fact, let \\(g_n \\in L\\) be a contractive sequence and write \\(g_n = u_n a_n v_n\\) for its polar decomposition. Then \\(\\theta(g_n^{-1}) = v_n^{-1} a_n u_n^{-1}\\) has the same radial component as \\(g_n\\), so that \\(\\theta(g_n^{-1})\\) is also contractive.\n\nWe can prove now the main result of this section.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn a conjecture of $λ$-Aluthge transforms and Hilbert--Schmidt self-commutators (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.04655)\nciteturn1academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Let $A$ be a complex square matrix, and write its polar decomposition as $A=U|A|$. ... $$ In 2007, Huang and Tam conjectured that the Frobenius norm of the self-commutator is contractive under $Δ_λ$: for every $0<λ<1$, $$ \\|A^*A-AA^*\\|_{F} \\ \\ge\\ \\|Δ_λ(A)^*Δ_λ(A)-Δ_λ(A)Δ_λ(A)^*\\|_{F}.\nTitle: On a conjecture of $λ$-Aluthge transforms and Hilbert--Schmidt self-commutators\nAuthors: Teng Zhang\nDate: Wed Mar  4 22:36:58 2026\n\nLet $A$ be a complex square matrix, and write its polar decomposition as $A=U|A|$. For $0<λ<1$, the $λ$-Aluthge transform of $A$ is defined by $$ Δ_λ(A)=|A|^λU|A|^{1-λ}. $$ In 2007, Huang and Tam conjectured that the Frobenius norm of the self-commutator is contractive under $Δ_λ$: for every $0<λ<1$, $$ \\|A^*A-AA^*\\|_{F} \\ \\ge\\ \\|Δ_λ(A)^*Δ_λ(A)-Δ_λ(A)Δ_λ(A)^*\\|_{F}. $$ If this inequality held, then the iterated self-commutator norms $$ \\Bigl\\{\\bigl\\|Δ_λ^{\\,m}(A)^*Δ_λ^{\\,m}(A) -Δ_λ^{\\,m}(A)Δ_λ^{\\,m}(A)^*\\bigr\\|_F\\Bigr\\}_{m\\in\\mathbb N} $$ would form a nonincreasing sequence and necessarily converge to $0$. In this paper we provide a counterexample, thereby disproving the conjecture. We also obtain the quantitative bounds $$ \\sqrt{\\frac32}\\ \\le\\ \\sup_{\\substack{A\\in\\mathbb{M}_n(\\mathbb{C}),\\ A^*A\\neq AA^*\\\\ 0<λ<1}} \\frac{\\|Δ_λ(A)^*Δ_λ(A)-Δ_λ(A)Δ_λ(A)^*\\|_F}{\\|A^*A-AA^*\\|_F} \\ \\le\\ 2. $$--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFlat matrix models for quantum permutation groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04456)\nciteturn1academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.7 years ago; Title: Flat matrix models for quantum permutation groups\nAuthors: Teodor Banica, Ion Nechita\nTitle: Flat matrix models for quantum permutation groups\nAuthors: Teodor Banica, Ion Nechita\nDate: Sun Feb 14 13:59:16 2016\n\nWe study the matrix models $\\pi:C(S_N^+)\\to M_N(C(X))$ which are flat, in the sense that the standard generators of $C(S_N^+)$ are mapped to rank 1 projections. Our first result is a generalization of the Pauli matrix construction at $N=4$, using finite groups and 2-cocycles. Our second result is the construction of a universal representation of $C(S_N^+)$, inspired from the Sinkhorn algorithm, that we conjecture to be inner faithful.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMinimal representations of a contractive operator 329 (https://acta.hu/download.phtml?id=2840)\nciteturn1search17 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Then \\(H=S^*S\\in K(B,C)\\), the triple \\((B_H,C_H;\\mathcal X)\\) is unitarily equivalent to \\((\\tilde B,\\tilde C;\\tilde{\\mathcal X})\\), with the unitary equivalence being given by the unitary operator \\(U\\) determined by the polar decomposition \\(S=UH^{1/2}\\). ... To establish the reverse implication, we have to show that up to unitary equivalence a minimal contractive multiplicative representation of \\(K\\) is of the form \\((B_H,C_H;\\mathcal X)\\) for a unique \\(H\\in K_{\\mathrm{core}}^{\\min}(B,C)\\).\nMinimal representations of a contractive operator 329\n\n**Lemma 3.2.** Let \\((B,C;\\mathcal X)\\) be a minimal multiplicative representation of the contraction \\(K\\), and let \\(S\\) be a pseudo-similarity from \\((B,C;\\mathcal X)\\) to \\((\\tilde B,\\tilde C;\\tilde{\\mathcal X})\\), where \\(\\tilde B\\) and \\(\\tilde C\\) are contractions. Then \\(H=S^*S\\in K(B,C)\\), the triple \\((B_H,C_H;\\mathcal X)\\) is unitarily equivalent to \\((\\tilde B,\\tilde C;\\tilde{\\mathcal X})\\), with the unitary equivalence being given by the unitary operator \\(U\\) determined by the polar decomposition \\(S=UH^{1/2}\\).\n\n**Proof.** Since \\(S\\) is closed and densely defined, the operator \\(H=S^*S\\) is selfadjoint (by [6] Chapter 5, Theorem 3.24). The operator \\(S\\) is injective, hence \\(\\langle Hx,x\\rangle=\\|Sx\\|^2>0\\) for \\(0\\neq x\\in D(H)\\), and therefore the operator \\(H\\) is positive. From Theorem 2.23 in Chapter 6 of [6] (see also formula 2.22 in the same chapter) we know that \\(D(H^{1/2})=D(S)\\). By the polar decomposition (see [6], page 334), we have \\(UH^{1/2}=S\\), where \\(U:\\mathcal X\\to\\tilde{\\mathcal X}\\) is a partial isometry with initial space \\(\\overline{\\operatorname{Im} H^{1/2}}\\) and final space \\(\\overline{\\operatorname{Im} S}\\). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTeodor Nicolae Banica - Independent Researcher (https://independent.academia.edu/TeodorBanica)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; # Teodor Nicolae Banica ... The work contributes to the architectural knowledge of the construction of the \"bath\" from Tomis,... more The work contributes to the architectural knowledge of the construction of the \"bath\" from Tomis, which has not benefited from a convincing publication since its discovery in the 60s.\n\n# Teodor Nicolae Banica\n\nadd Follow\n\nFollowers 14Following 252\n\nPublic Views\n\nI em an architect.\n\nless\n\n## Uploads\n\n### Papers by Teodor Nicolae Banica\n\nT.N.Banica.Notes on the urbanism of Tomis-the street network b\n\nARA reports, no. 16, 2025\n\nRecognizing the importance—and even the necessity—of having a general plan to accurately document... more Recognizing the importance—and even the\nnecessity—of having a general plan to accurately document the\nvestiges uncovered at a site like Tomis, we create